From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 05:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: decrypt inbound ESP in the PPE and deliver it to the CPU

Every inbound ESP packet takes the SOE CPU path: a QDMA TX request to
the SOE, a completion on QDMA RX ring 1 and a GRO hand-off. That path
decrypts 150 to 210 Mbit/s. The PPE can hand the packets of an SA to
the SOE itself: an outer ESP entry with the SOE fields (HOP0 = decrypt,
TUNNEL, ACTDP = SA index, TUNNEL_MTU) makes the PPE and the SOE decrypt
every packet of the SA without a CPU request.

Bind such an entry for every eligible inbound SA, with the CPU as its
destination: CDM1 queue 1, which also collects CPU reason 0x16 (hit
bind, force to CPU), on QDMA RX ring 1. The entry carries no part of an
inner flow: no TTL, VLAN, PPPoE or QoS channel, the MAC of the SA device
as DA and SA, and the special tag 0x72a2 in the etype slot, as the
vendor HWDOWN and MAP-E entries do. The CPU then applies the XFRM
policy, conntrack, the firewall and routing to every inner packet. With
the test-only predecessor of this code and the 1024 descriptors of RX
ring 1, one TCP stream reached 714 Mbit/s and four streams 643 Mbit/s
at about 50 % router CPU.

An inbound SA is eligible when it carries native ESP (no NAT-T), its
replay window is one that the engine implements (64, 128, 192 or 256
packets, for which airoha_soe_build_replay() sets ANTI_RPLY_EN), it
belongs to a GDM device rather than a DSA user port, and it has no
mark, interface ID, security context, care-of address or tunnel state.
Packet offload, IPv4 outer and tunnel mode are required for every SA.
An eligible SA is programmed with the SN, ICV and padding error drop
bits in addition to ANTI_RPLY_EN. Other SAs keep the CPU path and the
SA programming they had.

Default on. The module parameter soe_inline_rx defaults to 1. It takes
effect for SAs added while it is set; writing 0 keeps new SAs on the
CPU path with the SA programming of today, and SAs that already exist
keep their mode. The RX steering of decrypted frames is gated on the
count of eligible SAs, so it is inert while no eligible SA exists and
disarms once the last eligible SA has been replaced after a write of 0.

Binding. A learned packet (CPU reason 0x0f) of an eligible SA without
an outer entry asks a work item to bind one at the slot where the PPE
learned the outer tuple, at most once per 250 ms and not while the SA
conflicts with another SA of the same peer. The work item takes
soe->sa_lock and then flow_offload_mutex, the order of
airoha_soe_alloc_sa(), attaches a driver-owned FLOW_TYPE_SOE_INPUT_OWNER
entry to the SOE flow list of the SA and commits it through the SOE
bind barrier like every other SOE flow. SA delete, flush and forget,
and the flush after an XFRM policy change, remove it like any other SOE
flow; the next learned packet binds a new one.

The conflict rule that keeps an inbound SA off the PPE while another
inbound SA shares its outer addresses now compares the SPI of a
native-ESP SA, which is part of the PPE key of its 3T outer entry. Two
such SAs to the same peer no longer conflict, so a rekey overlap and a
peer with more than one CHILD_SA keep the PPE path instead of falling
back for the whole overlap or for good. NAT-T SAs, whose key has no
SPI, still conflict on the UDP ports.

Re-binding. The PPE ages an idle entry out after about 60 s and never
binds it again by itself. A learned packet of an SA whose entry is
bound, more than a second after its commit, means that the entry no
longer serves the SA: the work item reads the recorded slot, and if
that no longer holds the bound entry it marks the flow unbound. The
next learned packet of the SA then commits the entry again through
airoha_ppe_foe_insert_entry() and the bind barrier. A live bound slot
is left untouched, so a late learned packet no longer invalidates a
freshly committed entry and forces a needless re-bind; aging drives
re-binding instead. A slot read that times out leaves the entry bound
and is retried, rather than taken as an ageing-out. Every commit records
the slot that RX identifies the entry by, before and after the commit,
and drops the CPU requests of the SA that the bind barrier held back:
they are older than the packets the entry decrypts from then on, so
they would be reordered or fail the replay check of the engine.

RX. Once an outer entry has been attached, the RX path takes every
frame with hop flags 4 to 7 that is not a CPU-path completion, every
frame with the special tag and every frame that a recorded outer entry
forced to the CPU, before the GDM, DSA and LRO handling, as it takes a
CPU-path completion: the normal path would hand the plaintext to the
device that SPORT names without a secpath, and SW_UDF shares msg2 with
the LRO count. The SA is identified by SW_UDF, which carries the ACTDP
of the entry; PPE_ENTRY is used only to reject a frame whose two
identities disagree and to recover the identity when SW_UDF names
nothing. Only a frame with hop flags 4 and CPU reason 0x16 of a valid
inbound SA of this provider is delivered: it gets a secpath with the
state and olen 0, so the inbound policy check runs, a state and lifetime
check and the lifetime accounting, and enters the stack on the SA device
through GRO. Every other frame is dropped and counted and never reaches
the device that SPORT names. Frames dropped while their descriptors are
collected release no CPU request.

Replay. The engine checks every packet of an eligible SA against its
anti-replay window and drops the packets that fail the sequence, ICV
or padding check. Inline delivery does not advance the software replay
window of the SA, so software XFRM would otherwise accept a replay of a
packet that was delivered inline, on a path this driver does not
classify: another netdev, a VLAN or a stripped VID-0 tag, Wi-Fi. The
window of an eligible SA is therefore poisoned when the SA is allocated,
so that every software replay check of the SA fails before the packet is
decrypted. airoha_soe_build_replay() rejects ESN, so only the legacy and
BMP windows exist, and a stored sequence of U32_MAX with every bitmap
bit set rejects every incoming sequence in both. The driver's own RX
paths do not consult the window: the engine owns it for the inline and
CPU paths alike. As a second line of defence the ESP of an eligible SA
that this driver does see on its own RX path and would have handed to
software is dropped and counted: a full pending queue, no memory for a
request, a source other than the peer, a device other than the SA
device, an unavailable provider, IP options and outer fragments (the
first fragment carries the SPI, the others cannot be reassembled
without it).

CPU-path completions of an eligible SA skip the software replay check
and keep the state, lifetime and accounting checks, because the window
is poisoned and the engine owns it. This relies on the engine applying
the anti-replay window of an ANTI_RPLY_EN SA to the requests that the
CPU submits during an unbound window (start-up, after the entry ages
out, a conflict), not only to the packets that the PPE hands it. A
test-only probe checked that on an ANTI_RPLY_EN SA with a 256 packet
window: it submitted a copy of an inbound request with the same
sequence number, either right behind the original (three runs), 300 ms
later inside the window (one run) or 300 ms later and more than 11000
packets behind (two runs). The engine returned an error completion for
the copy in all six runs and never decrypted it. A replay of captured
ESP injected from the WAN side, while the outer entry is bound and
after it has aged out, has not been tested yet.

With the error drop bits set, the engine drops a failed request without
a completion, and the next completion of the SA takes the context of the
dropped request, so a software replay check on the CPU path would test
the sequence number of another packet. For the same reason
airoha_soe_error_work() no longer disables the provider when an error of
an eligible SA overlaps a queued request. To keep commits from leaking
contexts this way, a packet of an eligible SA that reaches the CPU path
within a second of its outer entry committing is dropped rather than
submitted out of order: its in-order twin was delivered inline, so it is
not a lost packet.

The flowtable could bind an inbound SA in the PPE from an offer of its
first inner flow, and that entry copied the egress, L2 rewrite and data
word of the flow, so every later packet of the SA went to that one host
without the inbound policy check, conntrack or the firewall. Refuse
every inbound SOE flowtable offer in airoha_ppe_soe_flow_add() and
count it. Inbound SAs stay PPE-disabled, so airoha_soe_ppe_flow_add()
refuses them as well. Learned packets of an eligible SA keep their
flowtable tag, so the flowtable can still offload the outbound
direction of their inner flow.

After an SOE fault the ESP of eligible SAs is dropped: the window is
poisoned and the outer entry is gone, and nothing else can decrypt it.
airoha_soe_fault_work() therefore expires the eligible SAs, so that IKE
renegotiates at once and, with hw_offload=auto, the peer installs a
software SA on the dead provider. The outage is about one IKE round trip
rather than a whole CHILD_SA lifetime. The counters are kept in the SA
and provider structures; this patch adds no debugfs.

Known limits: Outer-fragmented ESP of an eligible SA is dropped, so a
peer must not fragment ESP after encryption.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
diff --color -urNp a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -1263,6 +1263,7 @@ static int airoha_qdma_rx_process(struct
 #endif
 		struct net_device *netdev;
 		bool first_buf = !q->skb;
+		bool soe_inline = false;
 		bool soe_ring = false;
 		bool soe_pkt = false;
 		int data_len, len;
@@ -1307,13 +1308,26 @@ static int airoha_qdma_rx_process(struct
 #endif
 		len = FIELD_GET(QDMA_DESC_LEN_MASK, desc_ctrl);
 #if IS_REACHABLE(CONFIG_NET_AIROHA_SOE)
-		if (first_buf)
+		if (first_buf) {
 			soe_pkt = airoha_soe_rx_is_completion(e->buf,
 							      min_t(u32, len,
 								    e->dma_len),
 							      msg0);
-		else
+			/* The plaintext output of an inbound outer entry
+			 * must not reach the device that SPORT names without
+			 * a secpath, and its SW_UDF, which the LRO count
+			 * shares, carries the SA index. Take it before the
+			 * GDM, DSA and LRO handling.
+			 */
+			if (!soe_pkt && eth->soe_present &&
+			    airoha_soe_rx_inline_match(eth->soe, msg0, msg1)) {
+				soe_pkt = true;
+				soe_inline = true;
+			}
+		} else {
 			soe_pkt = !q->skb->dev;
+			soe_inline = soe_pkt && q->soe_rx_inline;
+		}
 #endif
 		if (unlikely(desc_ctrl & QDMA_DESC_DROP_MASK))
 			goto free_frag;
@@ -1387,7 +1401,9 @@ static int airoha_qdma_rx_process(struct
 			if (soe_pkt) {
 				skb_record_rx_queue(q->skb, qid);
 				q->soe_rx_msg0 = msg0;
+				q->soe_rx_msg1 = msg1;
 				q->soe_rx_msg2 = msg2;
+				q->soe_rx_inline = soe_inline;
 			} else {
 				rx_cb = (void *)q->skb->cb;
 				rx_cb->msg0 = msg0;
@@ -1441,6 +1457,16 @@ static int airoha_qdma_rx_process(struct
 					  q->soe_rx_msg2);
 
 			done++;
+			if (q->soe_rx_inline) {
+				/* No CPU request waits for this frame. */
+				q->soe_rx_inline = false;
+				airoha_soe_rx_inline_skb(eth->soe, q->skb,
+							 q->soe_rx_msg0,
+							 q->soe_rx_msg1,
+							 q->soe_rx_msg2, &q->napi);
+				q->skb = NULL;
+				continue;
+			}
 			if (!airoha_soe_rx_skb(eth->soe, q->skb, completion_tag,
 					       hop_flags, &q->napi))
 				dev_kfree_skb(q->skb);
@@ -1518,7 +1544,12 @@ free_frag:
 			u32 completion_tag =
 				FIELD_GET(QDMA_ETH_RXMSG_SW_UDF_MASK, soe_msg2);
 
-			airoha_soe_rx_drop(eth->soe, completion_tag);
+			/* An inline frame has no queued CPU-path context. */
+			if (soe_inline)
+				airoha_soe_rx_inline_drop(eth->soe);
+			else
+				airoha_soe_rx_drop(eth->soe, completion_tag);
+			q->soe_rx_inline = false;
 		}
 #endif
 		q->rx_drop_chain = !!FIELD_GET(QDMA_DESC_MORE_MASK, desc_ctrl);
@@ -1663,6 +1694,7 @@ static void airoha_qdma_cleanup_rx_queue
 	q->skb = NULL;
 	q->rx_drop_chain = false;
 	q->rx_frame_descs = 0;
+	q->soe_rx_inline = false;
 
 	while (q->queued) {
 		struct airoha_queue_entry *e = &q->entry[q->tail];
diff --color -urNp a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
@@ -228,7 +228,10 @@ struct airoha_queue {
 	/* First SOE descriptor metadata is kept while a scattered frame is built. */
 	int rx_frame_descs;
 	u32 soe_rx_msg0;
+	u32 soe_rx_msg1;
 	u32 soe_rx_msg2;
+	/* The frame being built is the output of an inbound outer entry. */
+	bool soe_rx_inline;
 
 	struct list_head tx_list;
 };
@@ -811,6 +814,21 @@ bool airoha_ppe_soe_is_quiesced(struct a
 void airoha_ppe_soe_bind_ready(struct airoha_eth *eth);
 void airoha_ppe_soe_bind_cancel(struct airoha_eth *eth,
 				struct airoha_soe_ppe_flow *flow);
+/* Result of airoha_ppe_soe_inline_check(): whether the recorded slot of an
+ * inline SA still holds its outer entry bound.
+ */
+enum airoha_soe_inline_check {
+	AIROHA_SOE_INLINE_CHECK_GONE,	 /* the flow was released meanwhile */
+	AIROHA_SOE_INLINE_CHECK_HELD,	 /* still waiting for the bind barrier */
+	AIROHA_SOE_INLINE_CHECK_BOUND,	 /* still bound in the recorded slot */
+	AIROHA_SOE_INLINE_CHECK_UNBOUND, /* aged out/invalidated → marked for rebind */
+	AIROHA_SOE_INLINE_CHECK_UNKNOWN, /* slot read failed; left bound, retry */
+};
+
+int airoha_ppe_soe_inline_bind(struct airoha_eth *eth, struct xfrm_state *x,
+			       u32 hash, const u8 *mac);
+enum airoha_soe_inline_check
+airoha_ppe_soe_inline_check(struct airoha_eth *eth, struct airoha_soe_sa *sa);
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_FS
 int airoha_ppe_debugfs_init(struct airoha_ppe *ppe);
diff --color -urNp a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -40,6 +40,10 @@ static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(ppe_lock);
 #define AIROHA_PPE_SOE_MAGIC_GDM4		0x729a
 #define AIROHA_PPE_SOE_PORT_AG			0x3f
 #define AIROHA_PPE_SOE_CHANNEL			2
+/* CDM1 queue of the decrypted output of an inbound outer entry. CPU reason
+ * 0x16 (hit bind, force to CPU) maps to the same queue, QDMA RX ring 1.
+ */
+#define AIROHA_PPE_SOE_INLINE_NBQ		1
 
 struct airoha_ppe_soe_info {
 	struct airoha_soe_ppe_info state;
@@ -757,6 +761,14 @@ airoha_ppe_soe_flow_add(struct airoha_et
 		return PTR_ERR(x);
 	if (!x)
 		return xfrm_act ? -EOPNOTSUPP : 0;
+	/* An inbound SA is decrypted by its driver-owned outer entry, see
+	 * airoha_ppe_soe_inline_bind(), or by the CPU path. No inner flow
+	 * may lend its egress to the outer entry of the SA.
+	 */
+	if (dir == XFRM_DEV_OFFLOAD_IN) {
+		airoha_soe_ppe_refuse_input(eth->soe);
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	}
 	if (!xfrm_act || xfrm_act->id != FLOW_ACTION_XFRM ||
 	    xfrm_act->xfrm.state != x || xfrm_act->xfrm.dir != dir ||
 	    xfrm_act->xfrm.policy_genid != tuple->xfrm_policy_genid)
@@ -1020,6 +1032,9 @@ airoha_ppe_foe_claim_entry(struct airoha
 		kfree(iter);
 	}
 	e->hash = hash;
+	/* A held outer entry commits where the PPE learned the tuple last. */
+	if (e->type == FLOW_TYPE_SOE_INPUT_OWNER)
+		airoha_soe_inline_set_hash(&e->soe_flow, hash, true);
 }
 static struct airoha_foe_entry *
 airoha_ppe_foe_find_learned_entry(struct airoha_ppe *ppe,
@@ -1301,6 +1316,11 @@ void airoha_ppe_soe_bind_ready(struct ai
 			continue;
 		}
 
+		/* Record the slot before the entry goes live; see
+		 * airoha_ppe_foe_insert_entry().
+		 */
+		if (e->type == FLOW_TYPE_SOE_INPUT_OWNER)
+			airoha_soe_inline_set_hash(&e->soe_flow, hash, false);
 		err = airoha_ppe_foe_commit_entry(ppe, &e->data, hash,
 						  e->soe_bind_rx_wlan);
 		if (!err && airoha_soe_ppe_flow_bind_finish(&e->soe_flow)) {
@@ -1570,6 +1590,15 @@ static void airoha_ppe_foe_insert_entry(
 	 */
 	if (state == AIROHA_FOE_STATE_BIND && !soe_entry)
 		goto unlock;
+	/* A matching SOE flow that is already bound owns this slot. A late
+	 * learned packet must not re-enter the bind path for it: bind_start()
+	 * fails for a bound flow and the entry would be invalidated, forcing a
+	 * needless re-bind and a window on the CPU path. Aging plus the inline
+	 * check drive re-binding instead.
+	 */
+	if (state == AIROHA_FOE_STATE_BIND && soe_entry &&
+	    READ_ONCE(soe_entry->soe_flow.bound))
+		goto unlock;
 
 	hlist_for_each_entry_safe(e, n, &ppe->foe_flow[index], list) {
 		bool bind_started = false;
@@ -1629,6 +1658,12 @@ static void airoha_ppe_foe_insert_entry(
 			}
 		}
 
+		/* Record the slot that RX identifies an outer entry by before it
+		 * goes live, so a decrypted frame that races the commit is still
+		 * attributed by PPE_ENTRY (claim_entry() records it again).
+		 */
+		if (e->type == FLOW_TYPE_SOE_INPUT_OWNER)
+			airoha_soe_inline_set_hash(&e->soe_flow, hash, false);
 		err = airoha_ppe_foe_commit_entry(ppe, &e->data, hash, rx_wlan);
 		if (bind_started) {
 			if (!err &&
@@ -2554,6 +2589,267 @@ void airoha_ppe_soe_forget_sa(struct air
 	mutex_unlock(&flow_offload_mutex);
 }
 
+/* The outer entry of an inline SA keeps the learned outer ESP tuple with
+ * HOP0 = decrypt, TUNNEL, ACTDP = SA index and TUNNEL_MTU, as the SOE fields
+ * of every inbound SOE flow, and sends the decrypted packet to the CPU on
+ * CDM1 queue 1. It carries no part of an inner flow: no TTL, VLAN, PPPoE or
+ * QoS channel, the MAC of the SA device as DA and SA, and the special tag
+ * AIROHA_SOE_INLINE_SPTAG in the etype slot, as the vendor HWDOWN and MAP-E
+ * entries do. The CPU then applies the XFRM policy, conntrack, the firewall
+ * and routing to every inner packet.
+ */
+static void airoha_ppe_soe_inline_compose(struct airoha_foe_entry *entry,
+					  const struct airoha_foe_entry *hwe,
+					  const u8 *mac)
+{
+	const u32 tuple_mask = AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_PACKET_TYPE |
+			       AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_UDP;
+	struct airoha_foe_mac_info *l2 = &entry->ipv4.l2;
+
+	memcpy(entry, hwe, sizeof(*entry));
+	entry->ib1 = (hwe->ib1 & tuple_mask) |
+		     FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_STATE, AIROHA_FOE_STATE_BIND);
+	entry->ipv4.ib2 = FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB2_PSE_PORT, FE_PSE_PORT_CDM1) |
+			  FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB2_NBQ,
+				     AIROHA_PPE_SOE_INLINE_NBQ) |
+			  FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB2_PORT_AG,
+				     AIROHA_PPE_SOE_PORT_AG);
+	entry->ipv4.data = FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_SHAPER_ID, 0x7f);
+
+	memset(l2, 0, sizeof(*l2));
+	l2->common.dest_mac_hi = get_unaligned_be32(mac);
+	l2->common.dest_mac_lo = get_unaligned_be16(mac + 4);
+	l2->common.src_mac_hi = get_unaligned_be32(mac);
+	l2->src_mac_lo = get_unaligned_be16(mac + 4);
+	l2->common.etype = AIROHA_SOE_INLINE_SPTAG;
+
+	if (FIELD_GET(AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_PACKET_TYPE, entry->ib1) ==
+	    PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV4_HNAPT)
+		memcpy(&entry->ipv4.new_tuple, &entry->ipv4.orig_tuple,
+		       sizeof(entry->ipv4.new_tuple));
+}
+
+/* Remove a linked outer entry that was not committed or was rejected. Called
+ * with flow_offload_mutex held. On failure the entry stays attached to its
+ * SA, whose teardown retries the invalidation.
+ */
+static void airoha_ppe_soe_inline_drop(struct airoha_eth *eth,
+				       struct airoha_flow_table_entry *e)
+{
+	bool quiesced;
+	int err;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&flow_offload_mutex);
+
+	quiesced = airoha_ppe_soe_quiesce(eth);
+	err = airoha_ppe_foe_flow_remove_entry(eth->ppe, e);
+	if (err) {
+		airoha_soe_ppe_adopt_quiesce(eth->soe, quiesced);
+		dev_err(eth->dev,
+			"failed to invalidate inbound outer entry; keeping IPsec PPE path disabled: %d\n",
+			err);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	airoha_ppe_soe_flow_release(&e->soe_flow);
+	if (quiesced)
+		airoha_ppe_soe_resume(eth);
+}
+
+/* Bind the outer entry of the inline SA of @x at the learned slot @hash. The
+ * entry is a FLOW_TYPE_SOE_INPUT_OWNER on the SOE flow list of the SA, so SA
+ * delete, flush and forget remove it like any other SOE flow, and it commits
+ * through the same completion barrier as every other SOE flow.
+ */
+int airoha_ppe_soe_inline_bind(struct airoha_eth *eth, struct xfrm_state *x,
+			       u32 hash, const u8 *mac)
+{
+	struct airoha_ppe_soe_info info = {};
+	struct airoha_flow_table_entry *e;
+	struct airoha_foe_entry *hwe, entry;
+	struct airoha_ppe *ppe;
+	bool bind_ready;
+	u32 index;
+	int err;
+
+	if (!eth || !eth->ppe || !eth->soe || !x || !mac)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	ppe = eth->ppe;
+	if (hash >= airoha_ppe_get_total_num_entries(ppe))
+		return -ERANGE;
+
+	e = kzalloc(sizeof(*e), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!e)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&e->soe_flow.sa_node);
+	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&e->soe_flow.soe_node);
+	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&e->soe_bind_node);
+	e->type = FLOW_TYPE_SOE_INPUT_OWNER;
+	e->hash = 0xffff;
+
+	mutex_lock(&flow_offload_mutex);
+	if (!rcu_access_pointer(eth->npu)) {
+		mutex_unlock(&flow_offload_mutex);
+		kfree(e);
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	err = airoha_soe_inline_flow_add(eth->soe, x, &e->soe_flow,
+					 &info.state);
+	if (err) {
+		mutex_unlock(&flow_offload_mutex);
+		kfree(e);
+		return err;
+	}
+	info.input = true;
+	info.tunnel_mtu = AIROHA_PPE_SOE_DEFAULT_TUNNEL_MTU;
+
+	spin_lock_bh(&ppe_lock);
+	hwe = airoha_ppe_foe_get_entry_locked(ppe, hash);
+	if (!hwe ||
